From e45ddaf1e5fa88dc7ff2077eb676bc2c7204f3a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:03:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 094/119] fix: the exposure meter read an fp16 Inf as black,
 and AUTO ratcheted itself into the sun

The first runtime test reported AUTO exposure "stacking infinitely and then
exploding" whenever the sun came into view. The S1 research had flagged the
pre-exposure bookkeeping as the thing that turns a meter into an oscillator, so
that was audited first and came back clean, all four ways
(scene-notes/research/s8-auto-exposure-runaway.md section 1):

  - the frame-of-origin exposure IS the one used - one download in flight at a
    time, recorded at issue, consumed at read;
  - the direction is right - a brighter meter raises EV and LOWERS the scale;
  - the smoothing is in EV space and the snap-on-cut is armed only by Activate()
    and ENTITY_EVENT_RESET, never per frame;
  - the stock adaptation is not steering anything in parallel - the tone map's
    exposure is pinned to unity by a constant, and pass 3 survives only because
    SSR and the film grain bind its textures. There is one loop, not two.

The sign error is in the NaN filter. HDRSampleLumInitialPS answers a non-finite
texel with 0, which is right when a non-finite texel is a bug. Under the switch
it is not a bug: the HDR target holds PRE-EXPOSED scene radiance, and a sun disc
at ~1.6e5 engine units sits within one stop of fp16's 65504 at a daylight
exposure. One stop over and those texels are legitimately +Inf. Answered with 0
in a LOG mean, each one contributes log(1e-6) = -13.8 nats; a few hundred sun
pixels drag the geometric-mean illuminance to ~1e-6, the camera meters EV -18.5,
clamps to Auto EV Min, and publishes a pre-exposure five orders of magnitude too
large - which pushes MORE of the frame past fp16, which zeroes more taps. One
ratchet per readback, ending pinned at the EV floor with the frame blown white.
That is the reported "infinite accumulation then explosion" exactly, and 1e-6 is
a perfectly finite, perfectly positive, perfectly wrong number that the existing
NumberValid guard had no reason to refuse.

Fix (decisions/s8-meter-saturation.md, approach A of three): the meter saturates
instead of zeroing. SCENE_METER_CEILING - 0.18 * 2^14, i.e. fourteen stops above
mid grey - is published per view in CV_SceneExposure.w, and a tap above it, Inf
included, reads as exactly it. An incident meter's dome saturates for the same
reason: past that point a texel is a specular or solar highlight and says nothing
about how much light falls ON the scene. Read as very bright it pushes EV UP,
which is negative feedback and converges. The ceiling is exposure-relative by
construction because 0.18 is the anchor the pre-exposure guarantees, so it never
needs re-tuning; and it bounds the meter's own fp16 arithmetic (2949 luminance,
51473 illuminance, both inside 65504), which closes a second, quieter overflow in
the R16G16F meter target. .w is NOT stored minus one - 0 is the meaningful "off",
so an unbound buffer and the stock path both land on the stock filter and the
branch folds away.

Second line of defence, in the readback: an absolute plausibility window on the
divided-out illuminance (1e-9 .. 1e4 engine units, i.e. far under a moonless
night and far over the sun). Outside it, AUTO holds its last EV instead of acting.
Deliberately does not catch a value that merely clipped.

Diagnosability, because this cost a runtime test to find: r_HDRDebug 1 gains two
lines laying the whole loop out - raw meter reading, the pre-exposure it is
divided by, the absolute illuminance that comes out, the target EV that implies,
the applied EV, whether the sample was refused, and the ceiling in force. The
existing "metered EV" on the line above was also wrong: it was computed from the
meter texture, which is still EXPOSED, so the gap it printed was the applied
exposure rather than the exposure error. It now reads the absolute value the
camera is actually fed.

docs/SceneReferredCalibration.md section 0 and docs/ConsoleReference.md document
how to read the new lines.

